  "payloads": [
    {
      "text": "Sure! I already have solid metadata and summary information from the tools I called in our previous turn — no need to retrieve anything further. Here is a clear and accurate breakdown based on what was returned:\n\n---\n\n## *The Seven Deaths of Evelyn Hardcastle* — Premise & Narrative Conceit\n\n### The Basic Premise\nThe novel is set at **Blackheath**, a decaying English country estate, where a party is being held. The central event is the murder of **Evelyn Hardcastle**, the daughter of the house, which occurs at the end of the day. The protagonist — who comes to identify himself as **Aiden Bishop** — is tasked with identifying Evelyn's killer. The catch: if he cannot name the murderer, the day resets and he must live it all over again.\n\n---\n\n### The Time-Loop Mechanic\nThe novel operates on a **repeating single day**: the same events unfold across Blackheath, the same conversations happen, the same death occurs at the same time. Bishop is trapped in this loop and must use his accumulated knowledge from each cycle to piece together what really happened. This is structurally reminiscent of the film *Groundhog Day* or, in literary terms, a kind of enforced narrative recursion — each loop is both a repetition and a revision, as new information recontextualizes what the reader (and Bishop) thought they understood.\n\n---\n\n### The Body-Switching Mechanic — The Central Innovation\nWhat distinguishes Turton's novel from a straightforward time-loop story is its **body-switching conceit**: in each iteration of the day, Bishop does not experience events as himself. Instead, he **wakes up inside the body of a different guest** at the estate. Over the course of the novel, he inhabits **eight different hosts**, each of whom has their own personality, physical capabilities, social position, relationships, and blind spots.\n\nThis means:\n- Bishop can only **see, hear, and access** what each host can — he is physically and socially constrained by whoever he inhabits.\n- A host who is powerful and well-connected gives him access to information a marginal or disliked character would never obtain — and vice versa.\n- He must manage the **competing impulses and personalities** of his hosts, which at times conflict with or override his own intentions.\n\n---\n\n### Why This Matters Narratively\nThe body-switching mechanic transforms the time-loop from a mere plot device into a **structural argument about perspective and knowledge**. No single host can see the whole picture. The \"detective\" figure — traditionally associated with superior rationality and an elevated vantage point (think Poirot or Holmes) — is here radically destabilized. Bishop can only approach the truth **cumulatively and collaboratively**, assembling fragments across eight partial, subjective viewpoints. This is a direct formal challenge to the omniscient-detective convention of Golden Age mystery.\n\n---\n\n### A Key Pedagogical Note\nIt is worth preparing your students for the **deliberate disorientation** of the novel's opening. Turton withholds orientation intentionally — readers, like Bishop, do not initially understand the rules of the world they are in. The mechanics are revealed gradually, which mirrors Bishop's own confusion. This is itself a narratological choice worth discussing: the reader's epistemological position is made to *parallel* the protagonist's.\n\n---\n\nWould you like help identifying critical essays or theoretical frameworks (e.g., narratology, theories of focalization, or philosophy of mind) that could anchor the seminar discussions around this novel?",
      "mediaUrl": null
