      "text": "Here is a comprehensive overview of *The Seven Deaths of Evelyn Hardcastle* by Stuart Turton, suitable for your graduate seminar module:\n\n---\n\n## *The Seven Deaths of Evelyn Hardcastle* — Background Overview\n\n### Publication\n- **Author:** Stuart Turton\n- **Published:** 2018\n- **Publisher:** Sourcebooks Landmark (US title); published in the UK as *The 7½ Deaths of Evelyn Hardcastle* by Raven Books\n- **ISBN:** 978-1-4926-6854-6\n- **Format:** Available in hardcover, paperback, ebook, and audiobook\n- **Length:** Approximately 116,000 words\n\n---\n\n### Awards & Recognition\n*The Seven Deaths of Evelyn Hardcastle* was one of the most acclaimed debut novels of 2018 in the crime and mystery genre. Based on available information, it won the **Costa Best First Novel Award** in 2018, a prestigious UK literary prize — a remarkable achievement for a debut. The novel was widely reviewed in major literary and popular outlets upon publication and has since been recognized as a significant work within its field.\n\n> ⚠️ **A note of scholarly caution:** While the Costa First Novel Award win is well-attested in general knowledge, the specific search results returned did not include a fully detailed awards list. I'd recommend cross-referencing with the Costa/Waterstone's prize records and databases like the Crime Writers' Association for a complete and citable list of nominations and wins for your seminar materials.\n\n---\n\n### Genre Positioning\nThe novel occupies a fascinatingly hybrid position in the contemporary mystery landscape, which makes it particularly rich for a seminar on **experimental narrative structures**:\n\n- **Country House Mystery Revival:** It is deeply indebted to the Golden Age tradition — most explicitly Agatha Christie — with its isolated English country estate (Blackheath), a large cast of suspects, and a single murder at the center. Critics have positioned it as a loving but subversive homage to that tradition.\n\n- **Time-Loop Structure:** What sets it apart is its science-fiction/fantasy mechanism: the protagonist relives the **same day eight times**, each time inhabiting the body of a *different guest* at the estate. This is not merely a plot device but a formal, structural feature that directly shapes the narrative's epistemology.\n\n- **Key Themes for Seminar Discussion:**\n  - **Recursive narrative** — how repetition with variation builds and destabilizes meaning\n  - **Perspective and identity** — the protagonist's sense of self is fractured across host bodies, raising questions about subjectivity and reliability\n  - **Moral responsibility** — complicity, agency, and guilt are complicated by the loop structure\n  - **The limits of knowledge** — each \"host\" has a different vantage point, foregrounding how understanding is always partial and positional\n\n---\n\n### Scholarly Relevance\nFor a module on **experimental narrative structures**, *The Seven Deaths of Evelyn Hardcastle* is a productive case study because it marries a highly conventional genre framework (the whodunit) with a formally radical conceit (multiple embodied perspectives across a time loop). It invites discussion of narratology (focalization, unreliable narration, temporal structure), genre theory (what makes a \"mystery\"), and reader experience (dramatic irony, withheld information, revelation). Its popular success also makes it a useful example of how experimental form can reach mainstream audiences.\n\n---\n\nWould you like me to dig deeper into any specific aspect — such as critical reception, comparative texts for the syllabus, or the narratological dimensions of its structure?",
      "mediaUrl": null
    }
  ],
