    {
      "text": "I want to be straightforward with you here, as scholarly accuracy matters especially for your teaching context. The tools I've used have returned only high-level, summary-level information — enough to confirm the broad outlines of the premise, but not enough for me to give you a reliably detailed, mechanics-accurate account of the time-loop and body-switching rules as they operate in the novel's internal logic. The search results keep cycling back to the same brief descriptions.\n\nHere is what I can **confidently confirm** from the retrieved sources:\n\n---\n\n### What the Sources Confirm\n\n- **Protagonist:** Aiden Bishop\n- **Central premise:** Evelyn Hardcastle is killed at a party at the Blackheath estate, and will keep being killed unless Aiden Bishop can identify her murderer and break the cycle trapping him.\n- **The loop mechanic:** Bishop relives **the same day eight times**, and each time he inhabits the body of a **different guest** at the estate.\n- **The goal:** He must solve the murder — identifying who kills Evelyn and why — before the cycle exhausts itself.\n- **The genre blend:** Golden Age country house mystery (à la Agatha Christie) fused with a time-loop structure, where **perspective is the central formal variable** — each host body gives Bishop a different vantage point on the same unfolding events.\n\n---\n\n### What I Cannot Reliably Confirm Without the Text\n\nThe finer mechanical rules — such as exactly how the loop resets, what Bishop retains between iterations, the precise nature of his relationship to his hosts' personalities and memories, who or what controls the loop, and what \"breaking the cycle\" actually entails — are details I cannot describe accurately without access to the novel's text itself. Giving you invented specifics would be a disservice for teaching purposes.\n\n---\n\n### My Recommendation\n\nFor a graduate seminar where you'll be describing the novel's mechanics precisely, I'd suggest:\n\n1. **Reading the novel directly** — it's 432 pages and the rules of the loop are embedded in the narrative itself rather than stated upfront, which is itself a narratologically interesting feature worth discussing with students.\n2. **Checking a detailed plot synopsis** on a site like SparkNotes, LitCharts, or a dedicated book club resource, which would have chapter-by-chapter breakdowns. I can attempt to fetch one of those pages if you'd like — just say the word.\n3. **Using the ambiguity pedagogically** — the fact that the rules are revealed gradually and incompletely is part of the reading experience, and framing it that way for students (rather than pre-explaining the full system) might actually serve your seminar goals well.\n\nWould you like me to search for a more detailed plot breakdown from a source like LitCharts or a review with spoilers included?",
